1
00:00:35,470 --> 00:00:39,910
Most of us encounter chemicals in one form or another every day.

2
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:44,830
They're used in a wide range of products and laboratory processes, and are often essential

3
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:46,810
in our jobs.

4
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:51,910
But if we don't take the proper precautions, many chemicals can be hazardous to our safety

5
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:53,910
and health.

6
00:00:53,910 --> 00:00:59,470
There are many types of potentially hazardous chemicals, flammables, corrosives, irritants,

7
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:05,070
sensitizers, toxins, even carcinogens, which can cause cancer.

8
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:10,350
Each of these chemicals has its own set of hazards, required safety precautions, and recommended

9
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:12,750
emergency procedures.

10
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:16,070
So how can we work with hazardous chemicals safely?

11
00:01:16,070 --> 00:01:18,590
How should we handle and store them?

12
00:01:18,590 --> 00:01:21,950
What personal protective equipment should we use?

13
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:35,380
And what should we do in an emergency?

14
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:42,380
For years, material safety data sheets, MSDSs, provided us with the answers to these questions.

15
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:48,100
MSDSs came about as part of OSHA's Hazard Communication Standard.

16
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:53,620
This right-to-know regulation required chemical manufacturers and importers to give their customers

17
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:59,420
MSDSs for any potentially hazardous products that they supplied.

18
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:04,560
But although certain types of information had to be included, OSHA did not require a standard

19
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:08,540
format for material safety data sheets.

20
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:15,420
As a result, MSDSs ended up in a variety of forms, with data arranged in different ways.

21
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:19,060
This often made it hard to find the information that you needed.

22
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:24,460
And in an emergency situation, this could cost valuable time.

23
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:28,500
Another difficulty with many material safety data sheets was that they were written in

24
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:33,620
technical language, as if they were intended only for health and safety professionals.

25
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:38,920
However, many other types of people, such as firefighters, emergency medical technicians,

26
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:45,920
and laboratory workers like you, also need to be able to understand what MSDSs tell them.

27
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:51,820
To address these problems, OSHA has adopted the Globally Harmonized System of Classification

28
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:57,140
and Labeling of Chemicals, GHS for short, into the HASCOM standard.

29
00:02:57,140 --> 00:03:01,840
The GHS was developed by the United Nations so that workers anywhere in the world would

30
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:06,800
be able to understand the hazards that a chemical presents.

31
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:12,780
A major part of the Globally Harmonized System is the use of the GHS Safety Data Sheet.

32
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:18,320
It gives chemical manufacturers a standard format and specific, understandable language to use

33
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:21,100
to describe a chemical's hazards.

34
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:26,680
Although compliance with the GHS is voluntary, most industrialized countries are adopting it

35
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:32,160
and will be starting to use GHS Safety Data Sheets soon, if they haven't already.

36
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:38,540
So it also makes it easier for U.S. chemical companies to sell their products internationally.

37
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:43,120
For many workers, the GHS Safety Data Sheet will look very familiar.

38
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:47,600
That's because it's formatted almost exactly like one of the versions of the ANSI Material

39
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,440
Safety Data Sheet, except for much of the information in Sections 2 and 3, whose

40
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,160
positions are swapped.

41
00:03:55,160 --> 00:04:01,520
Although just like ANSI MSDS's, GHS Safety Data Sheets present information in an as-needed

42
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:02,840
order.

43
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:08,280
The chemical's identity, its hazards and some emergency instructions are provided up front

44
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:11,200
for quick and easy access.

45
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:17,480
Basic safety information is presented next in easy-to-understand language.

46
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:22,120
Supporting technical data, as well as information for health and safety professionals is provided

47
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:26,000
later in the SDS.

48
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,620
Safety Data Sheets are designed to answer four basic questions that anyone who encounters

49
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:33,040
a chemical might have.

50
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:37,400
What is the material and what are its hazards?

51
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,800
What should I do if a problem occurs when I'm working with this material?

52
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:48,980
What precautions should I take to prevent problems when I work with this material?

53
00:04:48,980 --> 00:05:04,120
And finally, is there anything else I should know about this material?

54
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:10,080
Each of these questions is answered by one or more sections of the Safety Data Sheet.

55
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:15,960
For example, Sections 1, 2 and 3 provide answers to the first question, what is the material

56
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:19,480
and what are its hazards?

57
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:25,880
Section 1 of the SDS identifies the material using the standard GHS product identifier.

58
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:32,160
You'll find the same product identifier on both the SDS and the chemical's container label.

59
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:37,640
Section 1 also lists the name, address and telephone number of the chemical's manufacturer,

60
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:39,440
importer or distributor.

61
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:44,520
This is provided in case you have questions about the SDS or the material itself.

62
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:52,040
An emergency telephone number may also be listed for quick access to additional information.

63
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:56,120
Section 2 describes the hazards that are associated with the chemical, including the

64
00:05:56,120 --> 00:06:02,480
information that's provided on the chemical's label, such as the signal word, hazard statements

65
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:04,760
and precautionary statements.

66
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:10,480
It may also contain copies of the GHS pictograms that appear on the label.

67
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:14,560
Section 3 discusses the ingredients in the chemical and its composition.

68
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:21,360
It also provides additional identifying information such as its chemical identity, common name

69
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:34,840
and CAS and EC numbers.

70
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:40,120
The next important question that a safety data sheet answers is, what should I do if a problem

71
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:43,400
occurs when I'm working with this material?

72
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:48,080
Sections 4, 5 and 6 provide this information.

73
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:53,320
Section 4 of the SDS outlines the basic first aid measures that can be used before professional

74
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:56,680
medical assistance is available.

75
00:06:56,680 --> 00:07:00,840
Simple instructions are provided based on the chemical's route of exposure.

76
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:07,640
For example, first aid instructions for skin contact might be, remove contaminated clothing,

77
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:12,120
wash skin with soap and water, get medical attention.

78
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:17,200
If the material gets into your eyes, you might be instructed to immediately flush eyes with

79
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:20,080
plenty of water for at least 15 minutes.

80
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:23,840
Get immediate medical attention.

81
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:28,640
This section also provides information on the symptoms and effects the chemical can produce

82
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:33,520
to help you recognize if someone has been exposed.

83
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:37,760
You can see why it's important to know the appropriate first aid measures before you

84
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:43,840
work with a hazardous material, so be sure to read Section 4 of the SDS immediately for

85
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:46,680
any chemical that you are going to use.

86
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:51,320
You should also know the location of first aid kits, safety showers and eye washes in

87
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:55,120
your work area.

88
00:07:55,120 --> 00:08:00,760
Section 5 of a GHS safety data sheet provides information, precautions and instructions for

89
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:04,320
fighting fires that involve the material.

90
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:09,440
This section includes information on any hazards that the material could present if it burns.

91
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:13,960
For instance, a fire could release poisonous fumes that are more dangerous than the material

92
00:08:13,960 --> 00:08:16,840
itself.

93
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:21,660
Appropriate extinguishing media to use when fighting a fire involving the chemical is also

94
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:24,200
identified in this section.

95
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:32,280
This may include water, water fog, foam, carbon dioxide, halon and other dry chemicals.

96
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:37,880
Using the wrong type could make a bad situation even worse.

97
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:42,120
Firefighting instructions describing basic strategies that minimize the hazards the material

98
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:46,200
may present any fire are also provided here.

99
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:50,580
The personal protective equipment necessary for firefighting will be listed in this

100
00:08:50,580 --> 00:08:52,240
section as well.

101
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:57,360
This usually includes the full firefighting gear and an SCBA, self-contained breathing

102
00:08:57,360 --> 00:08:58,960
apparatus.

103
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:04,600
Even more protection may be necessary for some hazardous chemicals.

104
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:09,540
Section 6 tells you what to do in case of spills, leaks and other accidental releases

105
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:12,600
of hazardous materials.

106
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:18,040
Included in this section are general procedures for containing a spill or other accidental

107
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:23,660
release, cleaning up the hazardous material and decontaminating clothing and equipment that

108
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:27,560
comes in contact with the material.

109
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:34,520
To review, SDS sections 4, 5 and 6 provide information on what to do if a hazardous situation

110
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:46,260
occurs.

111
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:51,560
The next question the Safety Data Sheet addresses is what precautions can I take to prevent

112
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:54,160
problems when I work with this material?

113
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:59,400
For answers, we turn to sections 7, 8, 9 and 10.

114
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:03,700
Section 7 covers handling and storage practices that will minimize the physical and health

115
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:08,140
hazards of the material as well as preserve its quality.

116
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:12,600
As you would expect, the handling instructions on the SDS are often the same as the ones

117
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,440
listed on the chemicals container label.

118
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:20,380
For example, in this section you might see the caution, avoid contact with eyes, skin

119
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:26,220
and clothing, ensure that containers are properly secured before moving.

120
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:31,040
This section also provides information on the appropriate storage conditions for the material

121
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:38,360
and its container including temperature, humidity, atmospheric pressure, ventilation, vibration

122
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:41,260
in the area and exposure to light.

123
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:45,860
Depending on the material, these may all be relevant factors for safe storage and

124
00:10:45,860 --> 00:10:50,280
maintaining product quality.

125
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:54,880
Engineering controls, personal protective equipment and exposure guidelines are addressed

126
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:59,240
in section 8 of the SDS.

127
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:03,040
Engineering controls might include things such as the use of local exhaust ventilation

128
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:06,120
systems when working with some materials.

129
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:11,120
Other materials may require fully enclosed systems where no release of gases or vapors

130
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:14,040
can be permitted.

131
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:19,560
This section also lists the personal protective equipment necessary to minimize the risk of

132
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:21,120
exposure.

133
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:27,720
For example, to provide respiratory protection, the SDS may instruct you to wear a NIOSH approved

134
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:33,520
air purifying respirator equipped with organic vapor cartridges or canisters.

135
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:38,480
For eye protection, you may be directed to wear safety glasses with side shields or

136
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:40,960
safety goggles.

137
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:46,960
To defend against skin contact, rubber gloves and other protective clothing might be recommended.

138
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:54,240
Section 8 of the SDS will also list any TLVs, threshold limit values, PELs, permissible

139
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:59,840
exposure limits or other established exposure guidelines for the material or its hazardous

140
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:02,080
ingredients.

141
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:06,080
This information is used by your employer to determine the engineering controls and

142
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:10,760
personal protection appropriate for the work you do.

143
00:12:10,760 --> 00:12:16,560
Section 9 of the SDS describes the physical and chemical properties of the chemical.

144
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:22,160
This information is important for evaluating the use of a material for a specific purpose

145
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:28,040
and can help to determine what precautions should be taken when working with a chemical.

146
00:12:28,040 --> 00:12:32,520
Your employer will also use the chemical and physical characteristics listed in this

147
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:38,000
section to help determine the safest work practices for your facility.

148
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:42,700
The information can be helpful in identifying a chemical when its container label has been

149
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:46,240
damaged or destroyed as well.

150
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:51,920
Estability and reactivity is also information that is needed to determine safe handling,

151
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:55,920
storage, transportation and disposal procedures.

152
00:12:55,920 --> 00:13:00,720
For this data, we turn to section 10 of the SDS.

153
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:06,160
This section will indicate whether the material is chemically stable or dangerously unstable

154
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:07,880
under normal conditions.

155
00:13:07,880 --> 00:13:13,120
It will also describe conditions to avoid when working with a material such as heat,

156
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:20,660
pressure, shock or other physical stresses that might result in a hazardous situation.

157
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:25,340
If the material might react with another substance to create a hazardous situation such

158
00:13:25,340 --> 00:13:33,140
as poisonous vapors, a fire or an explosion, this incompatibility will be listed as well.

159
00:13:33,140 --> 00:13:38,000
This section of the SDS also addresses any hazards that could be produced as a result

160
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:46,800
of oxidation, heating, decomposition, polymerization or other chemical reactions.

161
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:52,960
Remember, sections 7, 8, 9 and 10 provide the information necessary to prevent hazardous

162
00:13:52,960 --> 00:14:05,750
situations from occurring.

163
00:14:05,750 --> 00:14:11,150
This brings us to the fourth question the GHS Safety Data Sheet is designed to answer.

164
00:14:11,150 --> 00:14:14,470
Is there anything else I should know about this material?

165
00:14:14,470 --> 00:14:19,470
This information can be found in sections 11 through 16.

166
00:14:19,470 --> 00:14:23,790
Most workers use this information under the guidance of health and safety professionals

167
00:14:23,790 --> 00:14:29,390
such as industrial hygienists, environmental managers and safety directors.

168
00:14:29,390 --> 00:14:34,230
However, your employer may also use these sections as a reference when setting up your

169
00:14:34,230 --> 00:14:37,030
company's standard operating procedures.

170
00:14:37,030 --> 00:14:42,810
So you might want to take a quick look at this information.

171
00:14:42,810 --> 00:14:48,290
Section 11, toxicological information, provides background toxicity information on the health

172
00:14:48,290 --> 00:14:50,290
hazards of the material.

173
00:14:50,290 --> 00:14:56,830
This includes likely roots of exposure, symptoms related to the chemical's toxicological characteristics

174
00:14:56,830 --> 00:15:01,250
and immediate and delayed effects of exposure.

175
00:15:01,250 --> 00:15:06,210
Section 12, ecological information, addresses the effects that the material may have on

176
00:15:06,210 --> 00:15:10,930
plants, wildlife and other aspects of the environment.

177
00:15:10,930 --> 00:15:16,370
Section 13, disposal considerations, provides information on safe and proper waste management

178
00:15:16,370 --> 00:15:19,370
options.

179
00:15:19,370 --> 00:15:24,970
Section 14, transport information, includes the chemical's UN number, proper shipping

180
00:15:24,970 --> 00:15:31,330
name, transport hazard classes, packing group and any special precautions that should be

181
00:15:31,330 --> 00:15:35,330
taken when transporting the chemical.

182
00:15:35,330 --> 00:15:41,290
Section 15, regulatory information, addresses any federal, state and international regulations

183
00:15:41,290 --> 00:15:44,610
that may apply to the material.

184
00:15:44,610 --> 00:15:49,170
Section 16 contains other relevant information that doesn't belong in any of the previous

185
00:15:49,170 --> 00:16:05,610
sections as well as information on preparing and revising the SDS.

186
00:16:05,610 --> 00:16:10,090
There's a lot we need to know about the chemicals we work with, and safety data

187
00:16:10,090 --> 00:16:12,530
sheets are where to go to find it.

188
00:16:12,530 --> 00:16:14,170
Let's review.

189
00:16:14,290 --> 00:16:19,970
GHS safety data sheets provide information about the chemicals you work with.

190
00:16:19,970 --> 00:16:27,890
SDSs are organized much like an ANSI-MSDS and contain much of the same type of information.

191
00:16:27,890 --> 00:16:33,770
The information in an SDS is presented in an as-needed order.

192
00:16:33,770 --> 00:16:40,680
Some of the information from an SDS also appears on a chemical's container label.

193
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:46,080
GHS safety data sheets are a valuable tool for working with hazardous chemicals, and

194
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:49,880
understanding them will help you to do your job safely.

195
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:55,600
So make sure you know where the SDSs are in your laboratory, and read them before you

196
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:57,800
use any chemicals that you work with.
